      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. Can polyurethane be applied directly over an epoxy coating?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Yes, applying an aliphatic polyurethane topcoat over a structural epoxy primer or intermediate build is a standard specification in industrial coatings. The underlying epoxy provides superior adhesion, chemical resistance, and dry film thickness, while the polyurethane topcoat provides excellent UV stability, flexibility, scuff resistance, and gloss retention. However, proper surface preparation, including checking the epoxy's re-coat window or mechanical abrading, is mandatory to ensure intercoat adhesion.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Why do epoxy coatings turn yellow and chalk when exposed to sunlight?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Most industrial epoxies are formulated using Bisphenol-A (BPA) resins, which contain highly aromatic ether rings. When exposed to solar ultraviolet (UV) radiation, these aromatic rings undergo photolytic cleavage and photo-oxidation. This chemical degradation breaks down the polymer chains at the coating's surface, leading to discolored yellowing and a powdery, white degradation layer known as 'chalking', which compromises the film's aesthetic and barrier properties.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What are the main chemical differences in curing reactions between epoxies and polyurethanes?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Epoxy coatings cure via an addition reaction where an epoxide ring undergoes nucleophilic ring-opening by an amine hardener, establishing highly rigid, rigid polyether crosslinked networks. Polyurethanes cure through a polyaddition reaction where a diisocyanate group reacts with a polyol (hydroxyl group) to form highly flexible, resilient urethane linkages (-NH-CO-O-) that display elastomeric behavior.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Which coating system is better suited for high-traffic industrial warehouse flooring?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              For heavy industrial warehouse floors subject to high wheel loads (e.g., forklifts) and high compressive stresses, a thick epoxy build is superior as a primer and intermediate binder due to its outstanding hardness, adhesion to concrete, and compressive load capacity. However, a thin polyurethane topcoat should be applied over the epoxy to protect the surface from scuffing, tire marks, impact cracking, and chemical stains.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. How does moisture impact the curing of polyurethane coatings?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Moisture can interfere severely with the curing of two-component polyurethanes. The highly reactive isocyanate groups (-N=C=O) react preferentially with water molecules (moisture) to form unstable carbamic acids, which rapidly decompose into carbon dioxide (CO2) gas and primary amines. The release of CO2 gas within the curing film leads to microscopic outgassing bubbles, pinholes, blisters, and reduced gloss, severely undermining the coating's structural integrity.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. What is the difference between aliphatic and aromatic polyurethane systems?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Aliphatic polyurethanes utilize linear, non-aromatic diisocyanates (such as HDI or IPDI) that do not absorb solar UV radiation, making them highly stable against weathering, yellowing, and gloss loss for outdoor exposure. Aromatic polyurethanes utilize aromatic diisocyanates (such as MDI or TDI) which contain benzene rings that undergo photo-oxidation. Aromatic PUs are highly tough and economical but will yellow rapidly in sunlight, restricting their use to primers, liners, or indoor applications.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
